WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:04.000
Dune is a movie with giant sandworms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.720
mysterious witches, and Timothee&nbsp;
Chalamet tripping balls in the desert.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:15.040
There’s spaceships and spice and Zendaya with blue&nbsp;
eyes, and almost none of it is explained. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:21.280
Dune’s characters have to watch YouTube videos to&nbsp;
understand the story. So what’s going on in Dune?&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.800
The movie is based on a book that was written&nbsp;
by Frank Herbert in the 60s. There have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.320
many attempts to adapt this book to movies&nbsp;
and TV and most of them.. aren’t very good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.560
Dune has been called “unfilmable” because the book&nbsp;
is so.. weird and complicated. The writing is full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.880
of internal monologue and thoughts instead of&nbsp;
action and dialogue. The plot is layered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:55.760
heaps of secret plans and hidden motivations. Its&nbsp;
worldbuilding is richly detailed with hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:02.480
words for its languages, cultures and religions.&nbsp;
Dune explores big ideas about consciousness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:09.120
politics, and philosophy. And the Dune sequel&nbsp;
books get increasingly strange and cerebral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.720
The new movie is a.. pretty close adaptation of&nbsp;
the first half of the first book. But it still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:20.320
leaves out a lot of important plotlines and&nbsp;
details. So how does the book compare to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.960
movie – what’s the real story of Dune? This&nbsp;
video has spoilers for the 2021 Dune movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.240
and for the first half of the first book. It has&nbsp;
no big spoilers for the next Dune movie or for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.760
the sequel books, but it does mention some small&nbsp;
lore details and quotes from the sequel books.&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:45.280
So Dune is set thousands of years in the future.&nbsp;
Humans live on many planets across the universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.640
And they’re ruled by the Emperor, Shaddam the&nbsp;
Fourth – we see the Emperor’s Herald in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:56.400
but we don’t see Shaddam himself. Beneath&nbsp;
the Emperor are the Great Houses, like House&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:03.440
Atreides and House Harkonnen – powerful political&nbsp;
dynasties who rule planets with armies and wealth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.000
The Houses meet in a congress called the&nbsp;
Landsraad, which balances against the power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.400
of the Emperor. The other big political power is&nbsp;
the Spacing Guild – cause the Guild controls all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.400
interstellar spaceflight. Guild navigators use&nbsp;
a drug called spice that lets them see into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:27.120
future – which is the only way to guide spaceships&nbsp;
safely across the stars. In the movie, these big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.720
Guild spaceships, called Heighliners, look like&nbsp;
tunnels that you can pass through to instantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:40.000
reach a distant planet – you can see another world&nbsp;
through this ship, it’s like a wormhole. The later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.800
Dune books describe this as folding space.
The Guild is “secretive” and mysterious – it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:53.200
hinted that Guild navigators have “mutated” and&nbsp;
“don’t look … human anymore”. So everyone kinda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.880
hates the Guild for being creepy extortionate&nbsp;
space weirdos, but everyone needs them to travel.&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.080
So for thousands of years, the universe has&nbsp;
been ruled by this balance of the Emperor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.120
Houses and Guild. They’re bound together&nbsp;
by a Great Convention, which has rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.840
banning the use of nuclear weapons. But a cheeky&nbsp;
bit of assassination and warfare is allowed within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:23.200
limits. Politics in Dune is a deadly game. Every&nbsp;
noble family watches their back for assassins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.240
and tests their food for poison. The&nbsp;
Houses rule their planets under a strict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:34.800
feudal class system – slavery, oppression, and&nbsp;
exploitation are common – this is an unfair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.560
unequal universe. The Houses have shares in&nbsp;
the CHOAM Company, which controls trade in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:47.440
the universe. It’s kinda like the Dutch East&nbsp;
India Company, or Amazon in space. CHOAM means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:53.440
money, and all the Houses fight for a slice. In&nbsp;
this struggle, a tiny shift in interest rates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:04:01.040
can “change the ownership of an entire planet”.
Science and technology is limited in Dune. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:07.840
there are giant spaceships and laser guns, but&nbsp;
there are strictly no computers, or robots, or AI.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:12.960
Cause thousands of years in the past,&nbsp;
people did rely on “thinking machines”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.720
but then some people used those machines to take&nbsp;
over the world and enslave people, and it sucked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.720
So then there was a religious crusade called the&nbsp;
Butlerian Jihad that destroyed all the computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:31.600
and banned them forever. In the Orange Catholic&nbsp;
Bible it is written “Thou shalt not make a machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:38.400
in the likeness of a man’s mind”. The idea is&nbsp;
that thinking and making our own decisions is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:46.080
what makes us human. Letting machines think for us&nbsp;
makes us less free, less conscious – so that’s why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.760
the machines were destroyed. Ever since, humans&nbsp;
have had to learn to think for themselves. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:58.240
founded schools to train the human mind and body&nbsp;
to its full potential. So now there are people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:05.360
called Mentats – “Human computers” who can think&nbsp;
super logically. They process data, calculate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:12.320
probabilities, and perceive peoples’ motives. The&nbsp;
Mentat Thufir Hawat works as a strategic advisor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:18.560
to House Atreides. In the books, Mentats take a&nbsp;
drug called sapho to make themselves smarter, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:24.960
the drug stains their lips red. In the new movie,&nbsp;
Mentats just have tattoos on their lips instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.160
Like the Mentats, the Navigators of the&nbsp;
Spacing Guild have trained their brains to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.200
calculate the mathematics of spaceflight.
And the other important school is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:41.280
Bene Gesserit, an ancient mystical order of&nbsp;
women with highly trained minds and bodies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:47.120
Their practice is influenced by Zen Buddhism,&nbsp;
with meditation and contemplation to achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:53.760
inner balance. Bene Gesserit can precisely control&nbsp;
all their muscles and nerves, even their body’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:59.600
biochemistry and their reproductive systems.&nbsp;
They have powerful memories and perception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.720
they can read peoples’ feelings through their&nbsp;
words and body language. They can control people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:12.000
with their Voice, they can fight in the weirding&nbsp;
way, they’re basically.. ninja nuns. The Bene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.480
Gesserit are named after the Jesuits, a real-world&nbsp;
Catholic order. Like the Jesuits, the Bene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:25.840
Gesserit do missionary work in distant places. And&nbsp;
they have political ambitions. The Bene Gesserit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:32.000
work with the Emperor and Houses, and they say&nbsp;
they live to serve. But the Bene Gesserit have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:38.160
their own plans – for centuries, these witches&nbsp;
have manipulated the universe from the shadows.&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:46.160
So Dune is about a galactic empire with&nbsp;
medieval politics. It’s a sci fi future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:52.800
that rejects technology, and instead focusses on&nbsp;
the power of the human mind. The author of Dune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:58.720
Frank Herbert was fascinated with thinking,&nbsp;
learning, and language. How we can expand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:04.400
our consciousness to better see the universe&nbsp;
– sometimes with the help of psychedelics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:10.080
Characters in Dune are constantly getting high,&nbsp;
expanding their minds and arguing about philosophy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:16.400
like college undergrads. So Dune is basically&nbsp;
like Game of Thrones, in space, on drugs.&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:23.200
And the most important drug is spice. Cause spice&nbsp;
not only makes spaceflight possible and can give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:30.160
visions of the future – spice also makes people&nbsp;
live longer, extending lifespans by decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:35.920
Many people are addicted to spice, and if they&nbsp;
stop taking it, they’ll die. It’s an addictive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.520
psychic, mind-expanding, life-extending,&nbsp;
spaceship-flying drug, and it tastes like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:48.240
cinnamon, so naturally spice is the most valuable&nbsp;
substance in the universe. And spice only comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:56.480
from one place – the desert planet Arrakis, also&nbsp;
called Dune. On Arrakis are giant sandworms whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:02.960
life cycle makes spice. Humans harvest the spice&nbsp;
and sell it, so whoever controls Arrakis becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.600
rich. The conflicts for resources in&nbsp;
the deserts of Arrakis are comparable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.720
to conflicts for oil in the Middle East.
For the last eighty years, Arrakis was ruled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:19.360
by the brutal House Harkonnen, led by the Baron&nbsp;
Vladimir. The Harkonnens come from the planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:25.920
Giedi Prime – “the oil-soaked, blood-soaked&nbsp;
hell hole of the Imperium”. The movie imagines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:32.160
this planet as being totally industrialised –&nbsp;
exploited and polluted until nature is gone, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:39.200
smog blackens the skies. The Harkonnens exploit&nbsp;
their people, too – they have slave labourers, sex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:46.400
slaves, and gladiator slaves to kill for sport.&nbsp;
The Harkonnens use and consume everything to feed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:53.840
their endless appetites for power and pleasure&nbsp;
– the Harkonnens are a mouth that swallows all.&nbsp;

00:08:55.600 --> 00:09:01.440
The Baron Vladimir is a ruthless political&nbsp;
schemer, who constantly indulges his desires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:07.760
for food and sex. He’s too fat to support his&nbsp;
own weight, so he uses suspensor technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:13.760
which lets him levitate. To rule Arrakis, the&nbsp;
Baron installed his nephew Rabban, who is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:21.360
“the Beast” for his brutality. Rabban is not known&nbsp;
for his intelligence – the Baron thinks of Rabban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:27.440
as “A muscle-minded tank-brain”. The&nbsp;
Harkonnens are advised by Piter de Vries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:34.800
a “Mentat-Assassin” who loves torture and poison.&nbsp;
Piter is addicted to spice, and this expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:41.280
habit forces him to stay loyal to the Baron. The&nbsp;
Baron uses peoples’ weaknesses to control them.&nbsp;

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:48.320
In the movie, the Harkonnens have a freaky&nbsp;
human-spider-thing as a “pet”, which is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:53.680
in the books, but according to interviews it’s&nbsp;
meant to be a messed-up science “experiment”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:10:00.960
this was a human who was made into a monster. In&nbsp;
the books there is a faction called the Tleilexu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:06.880
who do weird biology experiments, so maybe&nbsp;
they made this spider. Some fans theorise that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:13.520
this spider is Yueh’s captive wife Wanna, made&nbsp;
into a monster to torture her. But that’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:19.440
horrifying speculation and is not in the books.
Point is, the Harkonnens are dicks and the spice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:25.040
on Arrakis made them rich. But at the start of the&nbsp;
story, the Emperor orders the Harkonnens to leave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:30.880
and he gives control of the planet to House&nbsp;
Atreides, led by Duke Leto. The Atreides have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:37.440
a reputation for honour and integrity. They&nbsp;
protect their people and inspire loyalty in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:43.040
return. The Atreides are descended from&nbsp;
Atreus and Agamemnon of ancient Greece.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:49.280
And they rule the planet Caladan – a world&nbsp;
of oceans and jungles, farming and fishing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:55.440
The Atreides aren’t very rich, but Leto is popular&nbsp;
and influential among the houses of the Landsraad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:01.760
And the Emperor hates that – he sees Leto’s&nbsp;
popularity as a threat. So when Shaddam gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:07.920
them Arrakis, that seems like a good thing for&nbsp;
the Atreides. But really, Arrakis is a trap – the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:13.920
Emperor and the Harkonnens secretly plan to&nbsp;
destroy the Atreides – with an army of Sardaukar.&nbsp;

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:20.240
Sardaukar are the Emperor’s elite soldiers.&nbsp;
Renowned for being brutal and merciless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:26.400
they’re said to be the deadliest soldiers in the&nbsp;
universe. The Sardaukar come from Salusa Secundus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:32.160
a prison planet for the worst criminals in&nbsp;
the universe. Salusa is a “hell world” where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:38.720
only the strongest survive – and it’s this harsh&nbsp;
environment that makes the Sardaukar so powerful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:43.200
The Emperor doesn’t want the other houses&nbsp;
to know that he’s attacking the Atreides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:47.680
so in the book, his Sardaukar are&nbsp;
disguised in Harkonnen uniforms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:51.840
He and the Harkonnens want the&nbsp;
Atreides to “die in the dark”.&nbsp;

00:11:52.960 --> 00:12:00.160
The story begins with Paul Atreides – son of&nbsp;
the Duke Leto and Lady Jessica. Paul is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:05.600
fifteen years old in the book, and as the heir to&nbsp;
House Atreides, there’s a lot of pressure on him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:11.600
He has no friends his own age because of the&nbsp;
dangers of assassination. He’s highly trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:17.280
in politics, and in combat, and in the book&nbsp;
he’s trained as a Mentat – to have a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:24.080
logical mind like Thufir. Jessica trains Paul in&nbsp;
the way of the Bene Gesserit, to focus his mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:29.680
and body, his perception and memory, and to&nbsp;
use the power of the Voice to control people.&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:39.280
It might seem unrealistic to control someone&nbsp;
with your Voice. But author Frank Herbert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:44.960
said we do it “all the time” – like, it’s&nbsp;
easy to make someone angry with an insult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:49.040
or happy with a compliment. And with&nbsp;
practice, you can control people in more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:54.560
subtle and powerful ways – like politicians&nbsp;
and advertisers do. You’ve just gotta know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.560 --> 00:13:00.880
your audience, use the right words and&nbsp;
tone. Like and subscribe to Alt Shift X.&nbsp;

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:08.640
So Paul is young, but he’s a politician and a&nbsp;
warrior with a powerful highly-trained mind.&nbsp;

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:16.160
Leto visits the grave of his father, the Old&nbsp;
Duke. The Old Duke used to fight bulls for sport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:22.960
as a matador. Until he was killed by a bull in the&nbsp;
ring. The Atreides family keep the head of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:30.000
bull, with the Old Duke’s blood still preserved&nbsp;
on its horns. So the bull head represents facing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.400
danger, putting yourself at risk. Just like Leto&nbsp;
is putting himself in danger by coming to Arrakis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:42.320
Leto knows that the Emperor and the Harkonnens&nbsp;
plan to attack him. But he takes the risk anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:48.880
because he believes he can win. Leto plans to&nbsp;
use the “desert power” of Arrakis, by making an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:54.480
alliance with the Fremen, the local desert people.&nbsp;
In the book, Leto believes that the Fremen will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:59.920
great soldiers specifically because they live in&nbsp;
the harsh environment of Arrakis – just like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:05.280
Sardaukar are powerful because of the harshness&nbsp;
of Salusa Secundus. Author Frank Herbert was very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:10.560
interested in how environments affect people,&nbsp;
and how people affect their environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:18.240
So Leto bets everything on this plan to make&nbsp;
the Fremen his army to beat the Sardaukar. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:23.520
the movie, Paul says he doesn’t want to exploit&nbsp;
or oppress the Fremen like the Harkonnens did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:29.200
But in the book, the Atreides don’t care about the&nbsp;
Fremen like that – their goal is to exploit them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:36.240
The Atreides may seem like heroic good guys,&nbsp;
like House Stark in space, but throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:41.840
the book they use immoral manipulative&nbsp;
strategies, and we’re invited to question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:47.520
their morality. In the movie, Chani&nbsp;
asks “who will our next oppressors be?”&nbsp;

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:53.600
In the movie, Paul complains that he doesn’t get&nbsp;
to take risks and have adventures. He questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.600 --> 00:15:00.160
whether he even wants to be a duke and to lead the&nbsp;
Atreides. Leto is understanding, and says he loves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:06.720
Paul no matter what. It’s a touching moment&nbsp;
of unconditional love between father and son.&nbsp;

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:13.040
But in the book, we don’t see this kind of&nbsp;
softness. The Atreides are more formal and distant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:20.640
and serious, book-Paul does not question his duty&nbsp;
to their house. The Atreides in the book are an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:27.360
ancient dynasty in a deadly planetary conflict,&nbsp;
and they are not fucking around. While the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:32.404
humanises the Atreides, and makes them feel more&nbsp;
like a normal relatable family, showing affection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.404 --> 00:15:35.760
having breakfast, practicing mind control.
The Atreides get a visit from Gaius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:41.680
Helen Mohiam. Gaius is a Reverend Mother, a&nbsp;
special high-ranking Bene Gesserit who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:48.080
expanded her mind using drugs. Reverend Mothers&nbsp;
have access to the memories of their ancestors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:54.480
and of other Reverend Mothers. Gaius was Jessica’s&nbsp;
teacher back at the Bene Gesserit school. And now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.480 --> 00:16:00.160
she’s the personal Truthsayer of the Emperor&nbsp;
– he uses her for her ability to detect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:08.240
lies. So Gaius is a powerful, important person&nbsp;
and she has complicated loyalties to the Emperor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:15.440
and to Jessica and to the Bene Gesserit.
Gaius gives Paul a test. Paul puts his hand in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:21.600
a box that causes terrible pain – but if he moves&nbsp;
his hand, Gaius will kill him with a needle called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:28.720
the gom jabbar. So this is a test of self-control.&nbsp;
Someone without self-control, like an animal, will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:34.960
instinctively move away from the pain and will&nbsp;
die. To survive, you have to withstand the pain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:41.520
control your body and mind, master your fear and&nbsp;
your instincts. The Bene Gesserit believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:48.560
this ability to act consciously, and choose, to&nbsp;
think long-term not just in the moment, is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:55.440
separates humans from animals. They give this test&nbsp;
to everyone they train, so that only responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.440 --> 00:17:00.720
conscious humans gain the power of the Bene&nbsp;
Gesserit. Anyone who fails this test, they kill.&nbsp;

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:06.160
Before the test, we see the Atreides&nbsp;
bull head – cause like the Old Duke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:11.600
with the bull, and Leto with Arrakis,&nbsp;
Paul faces deadly danger in this test.&nbsp;

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:18.240
When Jessica was trained as a Bene Gesserit,&nbsp;
she took this test too. So she knows the pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:24.320
Paul is feeling and is terrified he might die.&nbsp;
To calm herself, she recites the Litany Against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.320 --> 00:17:30.800
Fear – “Fear is the mind-killer”, but the&nbsp;
fear will pass through me and I will remain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:36.480
The Bene Gesserit train to calm their emotions&nbsp;
and focus their mind. And Jessica’s training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:43.440
helps Paul pass this test. Paul is “human”,&nbsp;
he has self-control, so he withstands the pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:49.440
and lives. In the movie, he also has fiery&nbsp;
visions of the upcoming Harkonnen attack.&nbsp;

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:55.360
Then we learn about the Bene Gesserits’&nbsp;
secret plan. For thousands of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.360 --> 00:18:01.120
the Bene Gesserit have manipulated the genetic&nbsp;
lines of noble families like the Atreides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:07.440
They breed certain people with other people,&nbsp;
often by marriage to Bene Gesserits like Jessica.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:12.320
Even the Emperor has a Bene Gesserit&nbsp;
wife controlling his reproduction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:19.840
They select and combine certain genes, trying to&nbsp;
produce a superhuman called the Kwisatz Haderach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:26.800
This chosen one will be able see into the&nbsp;
future and past better than any Bene Gesserit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:32.320
Cause Reverend Mothers like Gaius can only&nbsp;
access the memories of their female ancestors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:38.480
The Kwisatz Haderach will access all memories.&nbsp;
He’ll be a male Reverend Mother whose powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:45.120
mind will guide humanity. The name Kwisatz&nbsp;
Haderach is borrowed from a real Hebrew term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:52.160
meaning to “shorten the way” between two places.
So the Bene Gesserit want to create the Chosen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:56.720
One. But Jessica has messed with their&nbsp;
plan. Cause the Bene Gesserit wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.720 --> 00:19:02.480
Jessica to have a daughter, not a son. That&nbsp;
daughter was gonna marry a Harkonnen, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:08.800
their child would’ve been the Kwisatz Haderach.&nbsp;
But Jessica, with her Bene Gesserit body control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:15.360
chose to have a son, Paul, instead, because Leto&nbsp;
wanted a son and Jessica wanted to make him happy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:21.680
She chose her family over her duty to the&nbsp;
Bene Gesserit. Jessica hopes that Paul will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:28.880
be the Kwisatz Haderach instead, but Gaius is&nbsp;
doubtful – their ancient plan is now uncertain.&nbsp;

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:36.800
So Paul might be the Chosen One, and he’s..&nbsp;
not happy about it. It feels weird and gross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:43.600
that he’s the product of the Bene Gesserit’s human&nbsp;
breeding program, their manipulative eugenic plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:50.160
In the book he feels an “offense” against his&nbsp;
“instinct for rightness”. He questions Gaius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:56.080
thinks she’s a “fatuous old witch” full of&nbsp;
meaningless “platitudes”. In other sci fi stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.080 --> 00:20:03.520
the hero’s magical mentors are good and wise&nbsp;
and pure like Obi-Wan and Gandalf the White. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:10.640
Gaius is a creepy witch in a black robe who works&nbsp;
for the Emperor who’s trying to kill Paul’s dad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:17.280
The Bene Gesserit are a shadowy political cult.&nbsp;
And their plan to artificially create the chosen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:24.320
one seems sinister and dark. In the book, Paul&nbsp;
feels that he is “infected” with a “terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:30.960
purpose” that he doesn’t understand. So what&nbsp;
is Paul’s destiny, and it really a good thing?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:38.480
Dune is about questioning the whole idea of&nbsp;
heroes, religions, politicians, predictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:45.440
and plans. It’s about the danger of anything&nbsp;
that reduces our ability to think for ourselves.&nbsp;

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:53.040
One of Paul’s teachers is Gurney Halleck.&nbsp;
Gurney is a legendary fighter, like one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.040 --> 00:21:00.160
the best in the universe. He’s also a musician&nbsp;
and a poet – a “minstrel-warrior” who plays an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:05.520
instrument called a baliset. They did film a&nbsp;
baliset scene for the movie, but it was cut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:13.520
In the book, Gurney often sings, and quotes poetry&nbsp;
and wise scripture and dirty lyrics. His charming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:20.560
words and music help the Atreides win allies and&nbsp;
influence people. Gurney is described as an “ugly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:28.160
lump of a man”. He's got a big red scar across his&nbsp;
face – and a burning hatred for the Harkonnens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:33.760
Cause in the book, Gurney was once a slave&nbsp;
of the Harkonnens. The Beast Rabban killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:39.440
Gurney’s family, and gave him his scar –&nbsp;
so Gurney wants revenge. Gurney says in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:44.480
the movie that the Harkonnens are so brutal&nbsp;
that they’re “not human”. The Harkonnens are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:50.080
often described as animals or beasts ruled&nbsp;
by their impulses – which contrasts with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:56.960
the idea of humans with conscious self-control.
Paul and Gurney train at fighting using shields.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.960 --> 00:22:04.240
Shields are force fields that can stop any&nbsp;
fast-moving object, but a slow blade can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:09.440
through. Guns and explosives are useless&nbsp;
against shields, so that’s why soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:15.520
in Dune fight with swords and knives instead.&nbsp;
The trick to shield fighting is to out-smart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:20.720
out-manoeuvre, and distract your opponent&nbsp;
so you can kill with a sneaky slow attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.280 --> 00:22:27.040
So like everything in Dune, even the sword fights&nbsp;
are smart and strategic, like a chess game.&nbsp;

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:33.120
Paul says that he’s not in the mood for fighting,&nbsp;
but Gurney says you must always be ready,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:38.160
no matter how you feel. So just like&nbsp;
with the gom jabbar test, this is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:44.480
self-control. To survive the deadly blade,&nbsp;
you must master your emotions, your body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:45.640
and mind. In the book, Gurney fights Paul so&nbsp;
aggressively that for a moment Paul thinks he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.640 --> 00:22:46.080
might be really be trying to kill him. Gurney is&nbsp;
warning Paul to be ready for anything on Arrakis].&nbsp;

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:53.280
One of Paul’s other teachers is Duncan Idaho, who&nbsp;
is also a legendary fighter. He’s a “Swordmaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.280 --> 00:23:00.560
of the Ginaz”, a “military genius”, he’s super&nbsp;
hot, and is fiercely loyal to the Atreides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:06.560
Duncan is a close friend to Paul, almost like&nbsp;
a big brother. In the movie, Paul tells Duncan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:12.560
about the dreams he’s been having of the future&nbsp;
– of this Fremen girl Chani, and of Duncan dying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:18.240
on Arrakis. These prescient visions are part of&nbsp;
Paul’s power as the potential Kwisatz Haderach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:25.280
In the movie, Duncan tells Paul not to focus too&nbsp;
much on dreams – he says “everything important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:32.400
happens when we're awake”. Cause Dune is about the&nbsp;
power of thoughts and visions, but it also warns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:39.680
us not to get too sucked in to our own minds, our&nbsp;
assumptions and plans. “the mystery of life isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.680 --> 00:23:47.600
a problem to solve, but a reality to experience”&nbsp;
– the deepest truths, and the joy and meaning in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:53.680
life, come from living in the present.
Before he leaves his homeworld,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.680 --> 00:24:01.040
Paul walks a Caladan beach. Caladan is a planet&nbsp;
of water to contrast the harsh dry deserts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:07.600
of Arrakis. Caladan is beautiful, plentiful, a&nbsp;
“paradise”. But the book says that the comforts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:14.640
of Caladan made the Atreides go soft. Life&nbsp;
was too easy, they got undisciplined. Dune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:20.800
says that people need adversity and hard times&nbsp;
in order to grow and achieve our potential.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:28.000
And Arrakis is the ultimate challenge. It’s a&nbsp;
test, like the gom jabbar, like the bull. Now Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:35.120
goes “into the fire”. Will Arrakis forge him&nbsp;
stronger, like the Fremen? Or will Paul burn?&nbsp;

00:24:36.160 --> 00:24:40.400
The Atreides arrive on Arrakis,&nbsp;
and this guy blasts a bagpipe solo.&nbsp;

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:48.080
When the people of Arrakis see Paul and&nbsp;
Jessica, they shout “Lisan al-Gaib”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:54.160
which means “the Voice from the Outer World”.&nbsp;
Because there’s a prophecy on Arrakis that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.160 --> 00:25:00.640
there will come a leader, the child of a Bene&nbsp;
Gesserit, who will lead Arrakis to freedom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:07.360
Paul and Jessica seem to fit this prophecy.&nbsp;
But thing is, this prophecy is a lie – it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:13.040
made up by the Bene Gesserit. The Bene Gesserit&nbsp;
have a whole system called the Missionaria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:19.680
Protectiva – for thousands of years, they have&nbsp;
spread specific religious ideas or “infectious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:26.960
superstitions” all over the universe. Particular&nbsp;
signs and sayings are planted in local cultures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:31.520
so if a Bene Gesserit is ever on one of these&nbsp;
planets, they can use those signs and sayings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:34.560
to gain power over people – by fulfilling the&nbsp;
prophecies that they invented. Dune author Frank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:39.840
Herbert said this was inspired by missionaries&nbsp;
in the real world, who use religion to manipulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:45.680
and exploit other cultures. So the people of&nbsp;
Arrakis hope that Paul and Jessica are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:51.840
chosen ones who will free them. But really,&nbsp;
the Lisan al-Gaib prophecy is a cynical tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:58.000
used by the Bene Gesserit to control people.
The Atreides move into their new home in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:04.080
city of Arrakeen. And Jessica hires a Fremen&nbsp;
woman called Mapes to be a housekeeper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:09.120
Not all the people of Arrakis are Fremen.&nbsp;
Like, the people of the cities and villages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:15.120
aren’t Fremen. The Fremen are their own specific&nbsp;
culture who live out in the desert – a “renegade”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:21.280
“will-o’-the-sand” people who live outside the&nbsp;
control of the Imperium. Fremen all have blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:27.760
eyes because their environment is saturated with&nbsp;
spice – and spice is coloured blue in the books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:34.000
Fremen have a reputation for being “fierce”&nbsp;
and “dangerous” – “They compose poems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:40.160
their knives”. The Harkonnens and the Emperor&nbsp;
see the Fremen as “scum” and “barbarians” who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.160 --> 00:26:45.360
“aren’t worth considering”. They think there&nbsp;
are only a few thousand Fremen living on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:50.320
edge of the desert. But the Atreides&nbsp;
think there could be lots more Fremen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:56.240
and that they could be more powerful than they&nbsp;
seem. So the Fremen are mystery at the start of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.240 --> 00:27:03.200
the book, and we gradually learn more about them.
Mapes has the honorary title Shadout, which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:08.080
“well-dipper” – cause water is super&nbsp;
important in the Fremen desert culture.&nbsp;

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:15.200
And Mapes isn’t just here for a job interview. She&nbsp;
came to test Jessica, to see if she really fits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:22.480
the prophecy of the Lisan al-Gaib. So Mapes shows&nbsp;
Jessica a crysknife, a blade made from the tooth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:29.440
of a sandworm. And she asks Jessica what is the&nbsp;
meaning of the knife. Jessica says it’s a “maker”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:34.560
and Mapes gets all excited, because the&nbsp;
Fremen call sandworms “makers”. So this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:41.920
shows that Jessica must know the secrets of Fremen&nbsp;
religion – she and Paul must be the Lisan al-Gaib.&nbsp;

00:27:41.920 --> 00:27:48.720
But thing is, in the book, Jessica is bullshitting&nbsp;
Mapes. Jessica doesn’t know the religious meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:54.560
of the crysknife. She was gonna say that the&nbsp;
knife is a “maker of death”, to mean it’s, just,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.560 --> 00:27:59.280
a weapon, but Mapes interrupted Jessica&nbsp;
once she heard what she wanted to hear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:05.840
Jessica then plays along and acts like she knows&nbsp;
this Fremen religious stuff, using signs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:12.640
sayings from the Missionaria Protectiva, but&nbsp;
inside Jessica knows this is a “sham”. Jessica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:20.560
exploits Mapes’ religion to manipulate her and win&nbsp;
her loyalty. Dune is about the danger of religion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:27.520
how it can warp our thinking and control us.
But this isn’t just about the Atreides using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:33.440
the Fremen. In the book, after Jessica leaves,&nbsp;
Mapes says to herself that Jessica is “the One”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:40.240
“Poor thing”. Mapes pities Jessica, and knows&nbsp;
things about her future. It’s hinted that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:46.560
just as the Atreides use the Fremen, the Fremen&nbsp;
may be using the Atreides for their own goals.&nbsp;

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:55.040
Paul is attacked by a hunter-seeker – a weapon of&nbsp;
assassination. It’s like a flying remote-control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.040 --> 00:29:00.560
needle – a “ravening sliver of metal” that&nbsp;
can burrow into flesh to strike the heart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:06.800
The hunter-seeker is controlled remotely by a&nbsp;
Harkonnen assassin. The camera on the seeker is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:13.360
bad, so it relies on motion to find its target.&nbsp;
So to survive, Paul has to stand perfectly still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.360 --> 00:29:20.320
while the deadly needle points at his face. So&nbsp;
this is just like the gom jabbar test – Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:26.960
faces a deadly needle, and to survive he has&nbsp;
to stay still, mastering his fear and his body.&nbsp;

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:33.440
Meanwhile, in the book, Jessica explores her new&nbsp;
home and she finds a mysterious door locked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.440 --> 00:29:39.440
a palm reader – which is a lock that only opens&nbsp;
to the shape of a particular person’s hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:44.240
So Jessica picks the lock by subtly&nbsp;
changing the shape of her own palm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:49.520
using her Bene Gesserit muscle control.
In other sci fi stories, characters might use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:56.080
hacking or magic or raw strength to break a&nbsp;
lock. But Dune is about the power of the mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.080 --> 00:30:01.840
so that’s how Jessica opens the door.
Inside, Jessica finds a conservatory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:08.560
A place full of lush plants and water on this&nbsp;
dry desert world. It’s beautiful, but it’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:14.640
terrible waste. This much water could sustain&nbsp;
a thousand lives on Arrakis, but instead it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.640 --> 00:30:20.240
used for the pleasure of the rich and powerful.
In the movie, there’s a scene with these palm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:26.320
trees, which are also a waste of precious water.&nbsp;
The gardener says that the waste is okay because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:32.320
these trees are “sacred”. But in the book, these&nbsp;
trees aren’t sacred. The people of Arrakis look on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:36.080
the trees with “envy” and “hate” for the waste of&nbsp;
water. The trees represent inequality in the book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.080 --> 00:30:41.440
But the movie takes away that moral complication,&nbsp;
and instead emphasises the mystical connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:47.200
between Arrakis’ people and its environment.
Anyway in the conservatory, Jessica finds a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.200 --> 00:30:53.440
hidden message from another Bene Gesserit. A&nbsp;
message so secret it’s written on a leaf in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.440 --> 00:30:59.520
braille. And it warns her that there’s a traitor&nbsp;
among the Atreides, working for the Harkonnens.&nbsp;

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:06.480
In the book, Thufir also gets a message warning&nbsp;
of the traitor. This message was planted by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.480 --> 00:31:11.920
Harkonnens, and they deliberately trick Thufir&nbsp;
into thinking that Jessica is the traitor. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:18.560
Harkonnens divide the Atreides, turn them against&nbsp;
each other, to distract from the real traitor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:25.280
Doctor Yueh, who is working for the Harkonnens.&nbsp;
When Thufir tells Leto, Leto doesn’t believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:31.440
that his beloved Jessica is plotting against him.&nbsp;
But he decides to pretend that he thinks Jessica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:37.360
is the traitor, to make the Harkonnens think they&nbsp;
tricked him, while he looks for the real traitor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:43.760
So the plot quickly gets complicated in the&nbsp;
book. Dune is full of these “plans within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:48.720
plans” and “plots within plots”. The story is&nbsp;
a chess game where only the best minds survive.&nbsp;

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:54.480
And there’s a sense from the start of the&nbsp;
book that Leto is doomed to fail. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:54.480 --> 00:32:01.040
he’s “a man snared by Destiny”, caught in an&nbsp;
inescapable trap. Leto has “death thoughts”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:07.120
thinking about how he’ll die. And Paul is&nbsp;
shocked to see this vulnerability in his father.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.120 --> 00:32:12.800
Paul sees that Leto makes mistakes, and he&nbsp;
realises that his dad won’t be around forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:20.080
Part of Paul growing up and becoming a man is&nbsp;
seeing the flaws and the mortality in his father.&nbsp;

00:32:20.800 --> 00:32:26.800
The Atreides takeover of Arrakis doesn’t go well.&nbsp;
The Harkonnens left behind broken equipment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:30.960
so the Atreides can’t harvest much spice&nbsp;
yet. And they’re still expecting an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:36.560
attack from the Emperor’s Sardaukar and the&nbsp;
Harkonnens. Leto’s great hope is the Fremen.&nbsp;

00:32:37.280 --> 00:32:42.720
Leto sent Duncan to try to make an alliance with&nbsp;
the Fremen. Originally, the Dune movie started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:48.400
with a scene of Duncan landing on Arrakis. So&nbsp;
Duncan meets the Fremen, and he learns that Fremen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:54.800
live in caves called sietches. There are millions&nbsp;
of Fremen living deep in the desert, way more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.800 --> 00:33:00.800
the Harkonnens and Emperor realise. The Fremen&nbsp;
are also technologically advanced – manufacturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:07.120
devices to help them survive in the desert. Duncan&nbsp;
also learns the Fremen are deadly in combat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.680 --> 00:33:13.280
So the Harkonnens and the Emperor are too arrogant&nbsp;
and prejudiced to see the potential of the Fremen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:13.280 --> 00:33:18.320
But the Atreides are open-minded and willing&nbsp;
to learn, which opens up new possibilities.&nbsp;

00:33:20.080 --> 00:33:24.640
So the Atreides meet Stilgar,&nbsp;
the leader of a Fremen sietch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:24.640 --> 00:33:31.840
The book emphasises Stilgar’s “aura of power”, and&nbsp;
his commanding voice – he’s an impressive figure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:37.760
Unlike the arrogant Harkonnens, Leto shows&nbsp;
respect for Stilgar and his people. So Stilgar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:44.240
shows respect for Leto by spitting on the table –&nbsp;
because in Fremen desert culture, giving moisture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:47.120
from the body is a sign of respect. Fremen&nbsp;
culture is shaped by their dry desert environment.&nbsp;

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:53.280
In the book, Duncan helped the Fremen fight some&nbsp;
Harkonnens. So Stilgar invites Duncan to join his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.280 --> 00:34:00.320
Fremen while also serving Leto – representing an&nbsp;
alliance between Stilgar and the Atreides. Stilgar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.320 --> 00:34:06.080
gives Duncan a crysknife, the sacred maker’s&nbsp;
blade, and it’s mentioned that the Harkonnens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.080 --> 00:34:11.840
are offering a huge bounty to anyone who can&nbsp;
bring them a crysknife. Because anyone who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.840 --> 00:34:19.040
a crysknife and has spice-blue eyes would appear&nbsp;
to be a Fremen – they could infiltrate any sietch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:24.320
And in the book, the Harkonnen Mentat-Assassin&nbsp;
Piter has blue eyes because of his spice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:30.640
addiction. So it seems that the Harkonnens want a&nbsp;
crysknife so that Piter can infiltrate the Fremen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:30.640 --> 00:34:36.240
so that he can kill the Atreides if the Atreides&nbsp;
hide with the Fremen. So this is another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:36.240 --> 00:34:41.840
deadly subplot hidden within the story.
But the meeting with Stilgar goes well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:46.320
so there’s hope for a larger alliance&nbsp;
between the Atreides and the Fremen.&nbsp;

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:52.640
The Atreides meet Liet-Kynes. Kynes is an&nbsp;
ecologist, or planetologist, working for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.640 --> 00:34:58.400
Emperor to study the environment of Arrakis.&nbsp;
She’s also the Judge of the Change – meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:58.400 --> 00:35:03.280
it’s her job to make sure everyone follows the&nbsp;
rules when the Harkonnens leave Arrakis and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:03.280 --> 00:35:08.640
Atreides take over. But secretly, the Emperor&nbsp;
has ordered Kynes to help betray the Atreides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.640 --> 00:35:14.880
and to let the Harkonnens attack them. But Kynes’&nbsp;
loyalties are even more complicated than that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:22.320
In the book, Liet-Kynes is a man. Kynes’ father&nbsp;
was Pardot Kynes, the previous Imperial ecologist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:28.880
And Liet-Kynes’s mother was a Fremen woman. So&nbsp;
Liet-Kynes was raised as a Fremen on Arrakis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:28.880 --> 00:35:35.760
Liet-Kynes married a Fremen, and they had a&nbsp;
child called Chani. Chani is Kynes’ daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:35.760 --> 00:35:41.760
in the movie as well as in the book. So Kynes&nbsp;
works for the Emperor, but Kynes is really loyal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:48.800
to the Fremen. Kynes leads the Fremen under the&nbsp;
name Liet. In the book, “Liet” and “Kynes” seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.800 --> 00:35:55.520
to be two different people at first. The Atreides&nbsp;
think the mysterious Liet might be the name of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.520 --> 00:36:02.080
Fremen god. It’s only later that we find out that&nbsp;
Liet the Fremen leader and Kynes the planetologist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:08.720
are the same person. So there are two sides&nbsp;
to Kynes’ identity – Imperial, and Fremen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:08.720 --> 00:36:16.240
Outsider, and native. Scientist, and leader.
Kynes gives the Atreides stillsuits. Stillsuits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.240 --> 00:36:21.840
are special outfits for the desert that cool the&nbsp;
body and stop it from losing moisture. All of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:27.520
your sweat, pee and poop, is captured by the suit&nbsp;
and reprocessed back into drinking water. Cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.520 --> 00:36:33.200
without a stillsuit, in the desert’s heat, you’ll&nbsp;
soon lose your moisture and die. The book goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:38.720
into lots of detail on the different technologies&nbsp;
people use to adapt to Arrakis’ environment.&nbsp;

00:36:39.440 --> 00:36:44.400
Kynes shows the Atreides how to wear their&nbsp;
stillsuits properly – it’s a difficult, technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.400 --> 00:36:50.480
thing for someone who’s never used one before. But&nbsp;
Paul somehow already knows how to wear a stillsuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:50.480 --> 00:36:56.400
properly. And that shocks Kynes, because it fits&nbsp;
part of the prophecy of the Lisan al-Gaib – “He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:56.400 --> 00:37:04.800
shall know your ways as though born to them”.&nbsp;
Paul’s personality and his words also fit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:10.560
prophecy. In the book, Kynes thinks of himself&nbsp;
as a rational sceptical scientist who doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:16.800
believe in superstitions. But Paul does fit the&nbsp;
prophecy. So it seems like there might be some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.800 --> 00:37:20.880
truth in the prophecy of the Lisan al-Gaib mixed&nbsp;
in with the Bene Gesserit’s lies and manipulation.&nbsp;

00:37:21.440 --> 00:37:26.880
The Atreides fly out to the desert to learn&nbsp;
about spice harvesting. These flying machines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:26.880 --> 00:37:32.000
are called ornithopters, which is a real&nbsp;
word for flying machines with flapping wings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:36.320
The book describes ’thopters as looking&nbsp;
like birds with feathered wings.&nbsp;

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:42.000
Harvester machines extract spice from&nbsp;
the sand using centrifuges. In the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:49.520
they look like giant beetles with snouts and&nbsp;
leglike tracks. The movie uses a simple industrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.520 --> 00:37:55.440
look for its technology, but the books have a&nbsp;
more fantastical and colourful 60s scifi vibe.&nbsp;

00:37:56.080 --> 00:38:00.800
The vibrations from spice harvesting always&nbsp;
attract sandworms. So when a worm comes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:00.800 --> 00:38:06.800
harvesters are flown to safety by a carry-all.&nbsp;
But this time, the carry-all breaks. In the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:13.520
it doesn’t turn up at all because of Harkonnen&nbsp;
sabotage. So Duke Leto heroically risks his life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.520 --> 00:38:20.480
to save the workers from the sandworm. This shows&nbsp;
that Leto genuinely cares about protecting people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:20.480 --> 00:38:25.680
Human life matters more to him than money, and&nbsp;
that’s what makes him different to the Harkonnens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.680 --> 00:38:32.160
But Leto isn’t totally selfless – he is a&nbsp;
politician. So in the book, he and Gurney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:38.000
give the workers a bonus payment, and he says it’s&nbsp;
for “public relations”, to make people like Leto.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:44.880
Kynes points out that Leto floods Arrakis with&nbsp;
propaganda, telling people to “Love the good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.880 --> 00:38:50.960
Duke”. And Leto admits that his propaganda&nbsp;
is a big part of why his men are so loyal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:50.960 --> 00:39:00.000
His image as a heroic leader is partly an act, a&nbsp;
“mask” for political advantage. So the Atreides do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:07.200
show some real heroism, but that image is partly&nbsp;
a cynical tool that they use to control people.&nbsp;

00:39:08.320 --> 00:39:13.760
In the book, the Atreides host a dinner party&nbsp;
with important people on Arrakis – businessmen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:20.160
politicians, smugglers – it gives the sense of&nbsp;
a larger world on Arrakis – this is a planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:24.000
with lots of different players with&nbsp;
their own plans and motivations.&nbsp;

00:39:24.640 --> 00:39:29.680
When the Harkonnens were in charge here, they had&nbsp;
a custom where they’d deliberately waste water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:35.760
dumping it onto towels on the ground, then&nbsp;
allowing poor people to beg for drops of moisture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:42.240
It was cruel, and demeaning. So Leto stops&nbsp;
this custom and gives beggars water instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:47.120
And Jessica declares that the conservatory,&nbsp;
that wastes so much water, will now be held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.120 --> 00:39:53.840
in trust for all the people of Arrakis. So again,&nbsp;
the Atreides do good for people, with gestures of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.840 --> 00:40:00.720
respect and generosity. Leto says he’s gonna&nbsp;
change things on Arrakis. Butdoes he really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:07.040
make any meaningful changes? Like sure, he gives&nbsp;
water to a few beggars, but he does nothing change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.040 --> 00:40:12.480
this fundamentally unfair feudal political&nbsp;
system. Right after banning the water custom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.480 --> 00:40:19.120
Leto just does a different water-wasting ritual&nbsp;
instead. So maybe these gestures are just more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:19.120 --> 00:40:25.200
propaganda to make the Atreides look good.&nbsp;
Dune is critical of politicians who appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:31.600
liberal and progressive, but are really just&nbsp;
aristocrats protecting their own power. Author&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:37.120
Frank Herbert said the Atreides are “admirable”,&nbsp;
but they act “with the same arrogance toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:37.120 --> 00:40:42.320
"common folk" as do their enemies”.
While the Atreides act honest and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.320 --> 00:40:48.000
honourable in public, they do some sneaky&nbsp;
bullshit on the downlow. Like, the Atreides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:53.760
forge signatures to take property and evict the&nbsp;
families of people who worked for the Harkonnens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:53.760 --> 00:41:00.160
The Atreides use Thufir, who is a “Master of&nbsp;
Assassins” whose ruthless methods bring “death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:00.160 --> 00:41:07.520
and deceit” wherever he goes. And Leto’s whole&nbsp;
plan here is to “exploit” the Fremen as his army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:14.720
and exploit the land for spice. Leto says that&nbsp;
he makes moral compromises for political gain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:20.320
He says the honourable Atreides banner&nbsp;
“could come to mean many evil things”.&nbsp;

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:25.680
So the Atreides are more moral than the&nbsp;
brutal Harkonnens. But their power is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.680 --> 00:41:32.720
still fundamentally based on coercion and force.
Like, this dinner party is fancy and glamourous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:38.960
but it’s also very tense – people are nervous&nbsp;
about getting poisoned – so inside a chandelier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.960 --> 00:41:44.000
there’s a hidden snooper device to detect&nbsp;
poison. The Atreides use secret hand signals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:50.480
to ready their soldiers during dinner.&nbsp;
This shows the fakeness of political power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:50.480 --> 00:41:55.840
Behind the formality and glamour there&nbsp;
is always the deadly threat of violence.&nbsp;

00:41:56.880 --> 00:42:02.560
Some of the most powerful people at the party&nbsp;
are the those who control water on Arrakis. Cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:08.160
everyone needs water to live, and on this planet&nbsp;
there’s barely any of it. Paul has to explain to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:13.920
someone what drowning is, and what boats are –&nbsp;
cause there’s isn’t enough water on Arrakis for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:21.280
anyone to ever drown or use a boat. So alongside&nbsp;
spice, water is a precious resource here. Author&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.280 --> 00:42:27.040
Frank Herbert said the value of water on Arrakis&nbsp;
is equivalent to oil. And the way this natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.040 --> 00:42:32.800
resource shapes politics and culture shows&nbsp;
the overarching, constant importance of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:32.800 --> 00:42:39.920
environment. No one is completely separated from&nbsp;
natural forces like water scarcity. Not the poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:39.920 --> 00:42:46.000
begging for drops of moisture, nor the politicians&nbsp;
who compete to control it. One of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:46.000 --> 00:42:54.320
important characters in Dune is the planet itself.
So everyone eats, and makes small talk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.320 --> 00:42:59.920
and secretly plots to destroy each other.&nbsp;
Jessica realises a Spacing Guild guy is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.920 --> 00:43:05.120
working with the Harkonnens. We learn that the&nbsp;
Harkonnens are smuggling lasguns to Arrakis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:12.720
Women try to seduce Paul. A water-seller bickers&nbsp;
with Kynes. We get a sense of mysterious power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:18.480
from Kynes, connected to Kynes’ relationship with&nbsp;
the Fremen. The Atreides talk about changing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:24.640
environment of Arrakis to give it more water and&nbsp;
plants, and Kynes acts weird and secretive about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.640 --> 00:43:30.960
whether this is possible. And this whole time&nbsp;
Leto is pretending to think Jessica is a traitor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:30.960 --> 00:43:36.400
and he’s super stressed about it. So at&nbsp;
this party, the plots thicken, the tension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:36.400 --> 00:43:43.280
rises, there’s a growing sense of danger to the&nbsp;
Atreides. All night, the bull head looms above,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:43.280 --> 00:43:49.520
with Atreides blood on its horns.
Later, in the book, Duncan gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.520 --> 00:43:55.840
drunk. He’s stressed, and homesick on this&nbsp;
dangerous alien world. So he gets wasted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:55.840 --> 00:44:02.720
and complains, and he reveals to Jessica that&nbsp;
Thufir thinks she’s a traitor. So Jessica finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:02.720 --> 00:44:08.240
understands why Leto has been acting weird and&nbsp;
distant with her, pretending to suspect her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:08.240 --> 00:44:16.720
So Jessica confronts Thufir. She says she’s not a&nbsp;
traitor, and she criticises Thufir’s Mentat logic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:16.720 --> 00:44:22.560
saying he is good at analysing the world around&nbsp;
him, but he fails to analyse his own thoughts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.560 --> 00:44:27.760
feelings – she says he’s biased against her for&nbsp;
being a Bene Gesserit, and that he’s jealous of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:27.760 --> 00:44:33.280
her closeness with Leto. She brags about her&nbsp;
power and uses her Voice on him – and Thufir&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.280 --> 00:44:35.440
is shocked to witness the true hidden power&nbsp;
of the Bene Gesserit. Thufir gets angry too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:35.440 --> 00:44:42.320
and considers killing Jessica. This in-fighting&nbsp;
is exactly what the Harkonnens want, and Jessica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:42.320 --> 00:44:48.560
realises that. But they fail to work together&nbsp;
because of their emotions and their differences.&nbsp;

00:44:48.560 --> 00:44:54.240
Jessica and Thufir are a Bene Gesserit and&nbsp;
a Mentat – both highly trained intelligent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:54.240 --> 00:45:02.400
superhumans. But no matter how smart you are,&nbsp;
everyone has human weaknesses – pride, jealousy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:02.400 --> 00:45:08.880
anger, fear, biases. Their emotions&nbsp;
overwhelm their rational minds. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:08.880 --> 00:45:14.800
Jessica and Thufir fail to find the real&nbsp;
traitor, and fail to save their family.&nbsp;

00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:21.680
Leto finds out about this fight between Jessica&nbsp;
and Thufir. And he feels bad about acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:21.680 --> 00:45:27.680
suspicious of the woman he loves. He regrets&nbsp;
that he never married Jessica. Cause technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:27.680 --> 00:45:34.480
Jessica is Leto’s concubine, not his wife. Leto&nbsp;
stays unmarried because it’s politically useful to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:34.480 --> 00:45:39.920
seem like he’s available for a marriage alliance.&nbsp;
But really he loves Jessica, and wouldn’t marry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:39.920 --> 00:45:46.000
anyone else. Leto goes to tell Jessica the truth&nbsp;
and to apologise about pretending to suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:52.480
her – but tragically, before he can tell her,&nbsp;
Leto is attacked by the real traitor – Yueh.&nbsp;

00:45:53.440 --> 00:45:59.440
So Yueh is the doctor for the Atreides family. And&nbsp;
in the book, the Atreides think it’s impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:59.440 --> 00:46:06.080
for him to be a traitor – because Yueh is a&nbsp;
Suk School doctor, with Imperial Conditioning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:06.080 --> 00:46:11.120
Imperial Conditioning is a kind of mental&nbsp;
training that supposedly makes it impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:11.120 --> 00:46:17.440
for a Suk doctor to kill or betray their masters.&nbsp;
This diamond tattoo on Yueh’s forehead is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:17.440 --> 00:46:24.160
mark of a Suk doctor. But the Harkonnens broke&nbsp;
Yueh’s conditioning – they captured Yueh’s wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:24.160 --> 00:46:30.720
Wanna, and they tortured her, to blackmail Yueh&nbsp;
into obeying them. So Yueh betrays the Atreides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:30.720 --> 00:46:37.440
but he does it to protect his wife. Yueh doesn’t&nbsp;
want to hurt the Atreides – he loves them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:37.440 --> 00:46:43.280
So throughout the book, Yueh feels conflicted,&nbsp;
guilty, and stressed keeping his secret.&nbsp;

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:50.080
Yueh thinks that after betraying Leto, he’ll&nbsp;
be remembered by history as a terrible villain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:50.080 --> 00:46:55.920
And he’s right. Each chapter of the Dune&nbsp;
book starts with a quote or excerpt from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:55.920 --> 00:47:01.760
in-universe history book written years after the&nbsp;
events of the story. They give us glimpses of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:01.760 --> 00:47:08.240
future historians think about these characters.&nbsp;
And in these books, Yueh is remembered as “a name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:08.240 --> 00:47:15.600
black in treachery”. “A Child’s History” says&nbsp;
“A million deaths were not enough for Yueh!”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:22.560
So this shows how history doesn’t capture the&nbsp;
full story of peoples’ lives. Yueh is a traitor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:22.560 --> 00:47:27.120
but he’s not some one-dimensional&nbsp;
villain – he’s a complex layered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:27.120 --> 00:47:33.520
character with his own feelings and perspective.
Earlier in the book, Yueh gives Paul an Orange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:33.520 --> 00:47:40.320
Catholic Bible. He thinks he gives Paul the&nbsp;
gift of religion before he betrays Paul – so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.320 --> 00:47:46.240
Yueh thinks, Paul will now go where Yueh cannot&nbsp;
go – meaning that Paul will go to heaven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:52.400
but Yueh thinks himself damned to hell.
Yueh talks with Jessica, which is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:52.400 --> 00:47:58.320
dangerous for Yueh. Because as a Bene Gesserit,&nbsp;
Jessica can read peoples’ feelings and detect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.320 --> 00:48:05.760
lies. So Yueh has to carefully control his words&nbsp;
and emotions to hide that he’s a traitor. He masks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:05.760 --> 00:48:12.000
his secret by expressing his real grief&nbsp;
for his wife Wanna. Jessica opens up too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:12.000 --> 00:48:18.080
and they really connect emotionally. So much that&nbsp;
Yueh almost decides to tell Jessica the truth that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:18.080 --> 00:48:23.760
he’s working for the Harkonnens. Jessica sees&nbsp;
that Yueh is hiding something from her, but she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:23.760 --> 00:48:29.760
decides not to investigate because Yueh is such&nbsp;
a good friend and she feels she should trust him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:29.760 --> 00:48:35.760
So this tragic, heartfelt scene makes us&nbsp;
feel sorry for Yueh before his betrayal.&nbsp;

00:48:36.400 --> 00:48:43.680
Yueh kills Mapes. He hits Leto with a paralysing&nbsp;
dart. He gives Paul and Jessica sleeping drugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.680 --> 00:48:49.280
so they can be captured. And Yueh turns off the&nbsp;
Atreides house shields, so the Harkonnens and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:49.280 --> 00:48:56.000
Sardaukar can attack. But Yueh also betrays&nbsp;
the Harkonnens. He installs a fake tooth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:56.000 --> 00:49:03.120
into Leto’s mouth – a weapon filled with poison&nbsp;
gas. He tells Leto to use it to kill the Baron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:03.120 --> 00:49:09.680
to get revenge for the torture of Wanna.
So the Harkonnens and Sardaukar attack. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:09.680 --> 00:49:15.840
the Atreides are shocked by how many soldiers&nbsp;
arrive. The Baron paid the Spacing Guild a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:15.840 --> 00:49:22.320
amount of money for troop transport. Like,&nbsp;
sixty years worth of Arrakis’ entire income.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.320 --> 00:49:28.720
The Baron has been planning this for a long time,&nbsp;
and he takes no chances to crush the Atreides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:29.280 --> 00:49:34.480
In the movie, the Harkonnens use these “slow&nbsp;
bombs” which decelerate so they can penetrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:34.480 --> 00:49:40.640
shields. While in the book, the Harkonnens use&nbsp;
old-fashioned explosive artillery against Atreides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:40.640 --> 00:49:47.440
men hiding in caves – it’s a surprise secret&nbsp;
weapon to use such ancient technology. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:48.240 --> 00:49:54.320
kill or scatter all Atreides on the planet,&nbsp;
and stamp out resistance by “cutting up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:54.320 --> 00:50:00.080
desert” with lasguns. The Harkonnens want&nbsp;
“total extermination” of the Atreides.&nbsp;

00:50:00.800 --> 00:50:06.960
In the book, there’s a scene where the Baron and&nbsp;
Piter have Jessica captive. The Baron had promised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:06.960 --> 00:50:13.280
to give Jessica to Piter as his prisoner or slave&nbsp;
– which is terrifying, because Piter is a sadistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:13.280 --> 00:50:21.120
torturer and killer. Piter is “a twisted Mentat”&nbsp;
– the Baron and the Tleilaxu have corrupted him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:28.800
made him “an animal” with violent selfish desires,&nbsp;
which makes Piter a less rational, less effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.800 --> 00:50:35.200
thinker. Like, the Baron convinces Piter not to&nbsp;
take Jessica, and instead promises him control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:41.920
of Arrakis – and Jessica sees that the Baron is&nbsp;
lying and manipulating Piter, but with his twisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:41.920 --> 00:50:49.760
Mentat mind, Piter doesn’t see this. And the&nbsp;
Baron fails to see how dangerous Piter is to him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:49.760 --> 00:50:56.240
So the Baron and Piter are both smart, dangerous&nbsp;
people, and this is their moment of victory. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.240 --> 00:51:02.960
Jessica sees that they’re kind of incompetent.&nbsp;
The Harkonnens’ minds are clouded and distracted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:02.960 --> 00:51:08.240
by their emotions and arrogance and greed.&nbsp;
They’re not focussed and disciplined like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:08.240 --> 00:51:16.800
Jessica and Paul with their powerful human minds.
The Baron wants Duke Leto and all his family dead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:16.800 --> 00:51:22.160
The Emperor and the Bene Gesserit are okay with&nbsp;
Leto dying. But they don’t want Jessica and Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.160 --> 00:51:27.440
killed as well. Cause Jessica and Paul are&nbsp;
important to the Bene Gesserit breeding plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:34.240
and under the rules of warfare in Dune, murdering&nbsp;
entire noble families is.. generally frowned upon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:34.240 --> 00:51:41.280
So the Baron can’t kill Jessica and Paul directly&nbsp;
– cause the Truthsayer Gaius could interrogate him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:48.400
and learn the truth. So instead, the Baron tries&nbsp;
to kill Jessica and Paul indirectly – he suggests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:48.400 --> 00:51:55.200
that Piter may choose to dump Jessica and Paul in&nbsp;
the desert, so the desert will kill them – so then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:55.200 --> 00:52:02.400
the Baron can they say truthfully that he didn’t&nbsp;
kill Jessica and Paul. The Baron twists the truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:02.400 --> 00:52:09.680
with word trickery. Dune explores how words and&nbsp;
language can be deceptive, and can limit our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:09.680 --> 00:52:15.520
thinking. So some of the most talkative characters&nbsp;
in Dune are the bad guys. Like, in the new movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:15.520 --> 00:52:22.000
the Baron doesn’t say much. But in the book, the&nbsp;
Baron never shuts up, he’s constantly indulging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:29.760
in theatrical, self-aggrandizing speeches.&nbsp;
While the heroic Atreides are men of few words.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:29.760 --> 00:52:36.080
In Dune, the deepest truths are not expressed&nbsp;
with words, but must be experienced first-hand.&nbsp;

00:52:37.040 --> 00:52:44.000
So Paul and Jessica are sent to the desert to die.&nbsp;
One of the guards is deaf, and Jessica is gagged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:50.400
so she can’t use her Voice power to control the&nbsp;
men. Paul is not as good at the Voice as Jessica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:50.400 --> 00:52:56.000
is, but he manages to command the men to ungag&nbsp;
Jessica, so she can use her Voice to control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:56.000 --> 00:53:01.840
and kill them. In the movie, Paul does some&nbsp;
cute footwork to trip a guy, while in the book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:01.840 --> 00:53:09.520
he kicks the dude so hard his heart stops. We&nbsp;
see Yueh’s diamond symbol here on the thopter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.520 --> 00:53:15.440
Because Yueh has pulled some strings to help&nbsp;
Paul and Jessica escape. He hides a Fremen desert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:15.440 --> 00:53:21.280
survival kit in the thopter. And he arranges&nbsp;
for Duncan to find them in the desert. So Yueh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:21.280 --> 00:53:27.840
is forced to betray the Atreides, but he also does&nbsp;
everything he can to help save Paul and Jessica.&nbsp;

00:53:28.800 --> 00:53:35.440
Yueh faces the Baron. The deal was that Yueh&nbsp;
would help the Baron defeat the Atreides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:35.440 --> 00:53:42.160
then the Baron would free Yueh’s wife Wanna,&nbsp;
let him join her. But the Baron just kills Yueh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:42.160 --> 00:53:48.480
so he and Wanna can “join” each other in&nbsp;
death – again the Baron twists the truth with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:48.480 --> 00:53:56.240
word trickery. So in the movie, it seems like Yueh&nbsp;
just failed, the Baron beat him – but in the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:56.240 --> 00:54:02.080
it’s different. Cause Yueh never really believed&nbsp;
that the Baron would give his wife back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:02.080 --> 00:54:08.560
He thought she was probably already dead. But&nbsp;
he needed to know for certain that Wanna is free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:08.560 --> 00:54:14.640
from her suffering. Wanna was a Bene Gesserit,&nbsp;
and she taught Yueh some truthsense skills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:14.640 --> 00:54:20.400
So by facing the Baron in person, and&nbsp;
reading his face, Yueh learns that yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:20.400 --> 00:54:26.480
Wanna is definitely dead, she’s free from&nbsp;
Harkonnen torture, and with that knowledge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:26.480 --> 00:54:34.160
Yueh can die satisfied. Yueh also succeeds in&nbsp;
helping Paul and Jessica escape. And gives Leto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:34.160 --> 00:54:40.880
the poison tooth to attack the Baron. Yueh thinks&nbsp;
that Leto was gonna die anyway, with the Emperor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:40.880 --> 00:54:46.480
and Harkonnens against him, so at least Yueh gives&nbsp;
Leto a chance to bring the Baron down with him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:47.120 --> 00:54:54.000
So Yueh feels that he succeeded – he outsmarted&nbsp;
the Baron and helped the Atreides – so with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:54.000 --> 00:55:01.360
final breath, he is defiant and triumphant.&nbsp;
History remembers Yueh as an evil villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:01.360 --> 00:55:08.480
but in a way his death is a heroic self-sacrifice&nbsp;
– dying for his Wanna, and for the Atreides.&nbsp;

00:55:09.840 --> 00:55:17.200
The Baron faces Leto helpless before him. He’s&nbsp;
wanted this for a long time. House Harkonnen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:17.200 --> 00:55:23.280
and House Atreides have hated each other for ten&nbsp;
thousand years – ever since an Atreides banished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.280 --> 00:55:29.440
a Harkonnen for cowardice after a battle.&nbsp;
Now the Baron can finally destroy his ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:29.440 --> 00:55:36.560
enemies. But in the book, the Baron’s victory&nbsp;
feels sour. He’s disturbed by Yueh’s defiance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:36.560 --> 00:55:42.480
And he finds out that Paul and Jessica have&nbsp;
escaped. He feels frustrated and uncertain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:42.480 --> 00:55:50.400
so he seeks comfort in food. Dune is about the&nbsp;
importance of self-control, mastering your mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:50.400 --> 00:55:57.440
and body. So the villain, the Baron, is someone&nbsp;
who’s totally controlled by his body’s desires.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:57.440 --> 00:56:04.560
The Baron constantly eats, he is “always&nbsp;
hungry and thirsty”. He has evil sexual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:04.560 --> 00:56:11.200
desires – in the book, the Baron drugs and&nbsp;
rapes slave boys. The Baron is so ruled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:11.200 --> 00:56:17.280
his urges he can’t even control the movements&nbsp;
of his body – the Baron’s hands are constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:17.280 --> 00:56:26.240
touching and fiddling compulsively. The Baron is&nbsp;
a beast full of hunger and lust, he’s the opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:26.240 --> 00:56:33.840
of the Bene Gesserit ideal of human self-control.
But while the Baron can’t control himself, he does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:33.840 --> 00:56:41.920
control others. He treats people like objects&nbsp;
to be used and exploited. He uses Yueh, he uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:41.920 --> 00:56:48.640
Rabban. He plans to kill Piter when he ‘outlives&nbsp;
his usefulness’. He thinks of the people of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:48.640 --> 00:56:56.000
Arrakis as “slaves”, no more than a “labor pool”.&nbsp;
He thinks of his men and of his enemies as “bees”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:56.000 --> 00:57:03.920
and “rabbits”, or as sheep and chickens, and of&nbsp;
himself as a “carnivore”. The Baron reduces people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:03.920 --> 00:57:10.720
to animals and objects, he dehumanises everyone&nbsp;
around him – and that’s what makes him the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:10.720 --> 00:57:18.320
ultimate evil in a story about the human spirit.
In Leto’s last moments, he remembers happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:18.320 --> 00:57:24.720
days with his family on Caladan. His last&nbsp;
thought is “The day the flesh shapes and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:24.720 --> 00:57:31.440
flesh the day shapes”, which suggests that our&nbsp;
experiences and environment make us who we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:31.440 --> 00:57:37.200
and that we make our experiences and&nbsp;
our environment in return. Leto feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:37.200 --> 00:57:43.040
interconnected with the universe when he dies –&nbsp;
there is a legend that in the instant he died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:43.040 --> 00:57:50.480
a meteor streaked through the sky on Caladan.
Leto uses his poison tooth to try to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:50.480 --> 00:57:56.720
the Baron. The gas kills Piter, but the Baron&nbsp;
survives cause he has his shield on. In the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:56.720 --> 00:58:03.440
he’s still hurt by the gas, so he needs to have&nbsp;
a medical black goo bath at a day spa to recover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:03.440 --> 00:58:09.040
The black goo isn’t in the book. But it is in&nbsp;
the old David Lynch Dune movie, so maybe it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:09.040 --> 00:58:14.960
a reference to that. Or it could be a reference to&nbsp;
this shot in Apocalypse Now. Or maybe a reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:14.960 --> 00:58:21.120
to this bit in It’s Always Sunny, who knows.
Paul and Jessica take shelter in a stilltent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:21.120 --> 00:58:28.080
And Paul’s consciousness expands. The effects&nbsp;
of the spice, of his training, the shock of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:28.080 --> 00:58:35.680
father’s death, it all comes together to unlock&nbsp;
a powerful new awareness. His mind now perceives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:35.680 --> 00:58:42.240
every detail of the world around him – he&nbsp;
processes and computes vast data – like a Mentat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:42.240 --> 00:58:49.920
but more. Paul has visions of the future not just&nbsp;
in dreams, but in waking awareness – the veil is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:49.920 --> 00:58:57.600
“ripped away to reveal naked time”. He doesn’t see&nbsp;
just one certain future, he sees many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:57.600 --> 00:59:04.800
possibilities – branching paths ever changing&nbsp;
and undulating like ocean waves. He doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:04.800 --> 00:59:11.360
see everything, much of the future is hidden to&nbsp;
him. But Paul sees many things, like that Jessica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:11.360 --> 00:59:19.120
is pregnant, and that her daughter will be “St.&nbsp;
Alia of the Knife”. Paul and Jessica will stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:19.120 --> 00:59:26.240
on Arrakis and their eyes will turn blue from&nbsp;
exposure to spice. Jessica thinks Paul must be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:26.240 --> 00:59:32.560
the Kwisatz Haderach of the Bene Gesserit plan.&nbsp;
But Paul says he’s something else, “something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:32.560 --> 00:59:40.720
unexpected”, “a seed” – and he again feels his&nbsp;
sense of “terrible purpose”. Paul sees a future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:40.720 --> 00:59:47.440
of religious war – of fanatical legions spreading&nbsp;
violence across the universe in the Atreides name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:47.440 --> 00:59:55.040
with him as their messianic leader. The book calls&nbsp;
this war a “jihad” or “crusade”, and connects it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:55.040 --> 01:00:02.000
to a primordial human need to spread our genes&nbsp;
across the universe – to evolve and diversify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:02.000 --> 01:00:10.320
species through chaos and upheaval. It's “war as&nbsp;
a collective orgasm”. Paul wants to prevent this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:10.320 --> 01:00:17.440
terrible violent future. And in the book, he’s&nbsp;
horrified by how own mind has changed. All this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:17.440 --> 01:00:24.000
knowledge and cold logic separates Paul from&nbsp;
his emotions. His father is dead, and he wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:24.000 --> 01:00:32.480
to grieve, but he feels nothing. His transcendence&nbsp;
takes away some of his humanity. Paul feels he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:32.480 --> 01:00:38.320
“a monster” and “A freak”, and he blames Jessica,&nbsp;
cause Jessica’s training is part of what caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:38.320 --> 01:00:44.720
this change in him. Jessica suddenly feels afraid&nbsp;
of this powerful mind that she helped create.&nbsp;

01:00:45.360 --> 01:00:51.120
In the book, Paul considers joining the Spacing&nbsp;
Guild, cause they would appreciate his powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:51.120 --> 01:00:57.920
prescient mind. But he decides not to, because&nbsp;
those guys are weird and gross. Instead, he says&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:57.920 --> 01:01:04.560
he and Jessica will join the Fremen, and they will&nbsp;
call him “Muad’Dib”, “The One Who Points the Way”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:05.120 --> 01:01:11.120
Muad’Dib is the name of this kangaroo-mouse which&nbsp;
Paul sees in the movie a couple times. The mouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:11.120 --> 01:01:16.400
is well adapted to survive in the deserts of&nbsp;
Arrakis. And the mouse has special meaning in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:16.400 --> 01:01:24.080
Fremen mythology because one of Arrakis’ two moons&nbsp;
has markings that look like the Muad’Dib mouse.&nbsp;

01:01:24.080 --> 01:01:31.920
So Paul emerges from the womb of the tent reborn.&nbsp;
He has transformed and expanded his mind. And he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:31.920 --> 01:01:40.640
is no longer a child but as a man, the new Duke&nbsp;
Atreides. Now, at last, Paul can mourn his father.&nbsp;

01:01:41.760 --> 01:01:46.800
Paul and Jessica meet up with Duncan, and&nbsp;
they take refuge with Kynes. The Emperor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:46.800 --> 01:01:52.400
ordered Kynes to help destroy the Atreides, so&nbsp;
Kynes is taking a huge risk by protecting them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:52.400 --> 01:01:59.520
But Kynes saw Leto’s heroism and leadership.&nbsp;
Kynes saw that Paul fits the Fremen prophecies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:59.520 --> 01:02:04.640
And Kynes saw that the Atreides are&nbsp;
interested in changing Arrakis’ environment.&nbsp;

01:02:05.440 --> 01:02:11.680
Kynes takes the Atreides to an old ecological&nbsp;
station – a science lab to study the environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:11.680 --> 01:02:18.480
of Arrakis. The book explains that Kynes’ father,&nbsp;
Pardot, taught the Fremen about ecology – the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:18.480 --> 01:02:25.680
systems of life, climate, water, animals. Pardot&nbsp;
and the Fremen made a plan to transform the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:25.680 --> 01:02:33.360
environment of Arrakis, to make it a green planet&nbsp;
full of water and plants. After Pardot died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:33.360 --> 01:02:38.800
Liet-Kynes and the Fremen have continued to change&nbsp;
Arrakis – they’ve been planting vegetation in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:38.800 --> 01:02:46.320
desert, feeding it moisture harvested from the&nbsp;
wind, starting oases of life and water, to reshape&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:46.320 --> 01:02:52.480
the desert. And they’re doing this secretly. The&nbsp;
Fremen have been bribing the Spacing Guild with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:52.480 --> 01:02:59.200
spice to hide Fremen activity from satellites.
Paul tells Kynes he could help transform&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:59.200 --> 01:03:06.960
Arrakis – if Paul becomes Emperor of the universe.&nbsp;
With his new consciousness and confidence, Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:06.960 --> 01:03:12.960
plans a play for the throne. If Paul can prove&nbsp;
to other Houses of the Landsraad that the Emperor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:12.960 --> 01:03:17.280
attacked the Atreides, everyone would turn&nbsp;
against the Emperor. That’s the whole point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:17.280 --> 01:03:24.480
of the Landsraad – it limits the Emperor’s power,&nbsp;
to protect the Houses. So Paul plans to blackmail&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:24.480 --> 01:03:30.400
the Emperor, and marry one of the Emperor’s&nbsp;
daughters, to take the throne himself. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:30.400 --> 01:03:37.680
Paul could help Kynes change Arrakis.
So Paul shows confidence and authority. But in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:37.680 --> 01:03:45.200
book, he also shows humility and loyalty to Kynes&nbsp;
– he says he’d give his life for Kynes – showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:45.200 --> 01:03:50.400
that Atreides heroism that makes them compelling&nbsp;
leaders. And so Kynes becomes loyal to Paul.&nbsp;

01:03:51.600 --> 01:03:57.920
Then the Sardaukar attack, coming to capture or&nbsp;
kill the Atreides. In the movie, the Sardaukar cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:57.920 --> 01:04:04.240
through a door with a lasgun. Lasguns are powerful&nbsp;
laser weapons, but they’re also super dangerous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:04.240 --> 01:04:11.040
Cause if a laser beam touches a shield, the&nbsp;
shield and the lasgun explode. Like, a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:11.040 --> 01:04:17.040
nuclear explosion, which kills the lasgun user&nbsp;
as well as their target. Earlier in the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:17.040 --> 01:04:22.080
Duncan leaves a shield as a trap to blow up&nbsp;
a bunch of Sardaukar. So because of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:22.080 --> 01:04:26.640
the Sardaukar avoid using lasguns here, cause&nbsp;
they’re scared of hitting a shield again.&nbsp;

01:04:27.200 --> 01:04:32.560
To give Paul and Jessica time to escape,&nbsp;
Duncan heroically holds off the Sardaukar&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:32.560 --> 01:04:39.680
and is killed. Just like in Paul’s dream earlier.&nbsp;
So this shows that Paul’s prescient visions can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:39.680 --> 01:04:47.200
come true. Will the holy war come true as well?
Kynes Paul and Jessica escape through a secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:47.200 --> 01:04:52.240
exit. In the book, they go through a&nbsp;
whole maze with lights that guide the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:52.240 --> 01:04:56.160
way but then extinguish, so that&nbsp;
the Sardaukar can’t follow them.&nbsp;

01:04:56.720 --> 01:05:03.120
In the movie, Kynes heads to the desert and uses&nbsp;
a device called a thumper to attract a sandworm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:03.120 --> 01:05:09.200
With these maker hooks, Kynes plans to mount&nbsp;
and ride a sandworm to travel, which is one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:09.200 --> 01:05:15.760
the great secrets of Fremen power. But Kynes is&nbsp;
attacked by Sardaukar – because Kynes disobeyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:15.760 --> 01:05:22.800
the Emperor. Kynes defiantly says that her&nbsp;
true master is Shai-Hulud – which is the Fremen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:22.800 --> 01:05:30.000
religious name for the sandworms – “Old Father&nbsp;
Eternity”. Kynes pounds the sand like a thumper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:37.040
so that she and the Sardaukar are eaten by her&nbsp;
god. It’s.. extremely badass, and demonstrates&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:37.040 --> 01:05:42.880
the power of the Fremen and the sandworms.
But in the book, and in the old TV miniseries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:43.451 --> 01:05:49.359
Kynes’ death is very different. The Harkonnens&nbsp;
leave Kynes to die in the desert. He becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:49.360 --> 01:05:55.840
delirious, and has hallucinations of his father&nbsp;
Pardot. Pardot lectures Liet-Kynes about their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:55.840 --> 01:06:02.080
plan to change Arrakis, with all these endless&nbsp;
scientific facts and theories to control the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:02.080 --> 01:06:07.920
environment. But while Kynes thinks about&nbsp;
controlling the environment, the environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:07.920 --> 01:06:15.520
is killing him – he’s burning in the sun, birds&nbsp;
circle above to eat him. And in the end, Kynes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:15.520 --> 01:06:22.720
is killed by a spiceblow – which is the explosive&nbsp;
chemical reaction that creates spice on Arrakis.&nbsp;

01:06:22.720 --> 01:06:28.960
So Kynes and his father spent their lives&nbsp;
trying to master this planet. But Kynes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:28.960 --> 01:06:35.680
gets killed by the planet he tried to control.&nbsp;
His knowledge and his science can’t save him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:36.240 --> 01:06:43.520
This shows the limits of human knowledge&nbsp;
and plans. Environmental forces, the complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:43.520 --> 01:06:49.760
ever-changing machinations of the universe,&nbsp;
are bigger and more chaotic than any person&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:49.760 --> 01:06:58.480
can control. No plan or science can tame the&nbsp;
universe – nature always has the last word.&nbsp;

01:06:59.520 --> 01:07:05.280
Paul and Jessica escape by flying into&nbsp;
a storm. Arrakis dust-storms are deadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:05.280 --> 01:07:12.880
with winds that can “cut metal like butter” and&nbsp;
strip flesh to bone. It should be certain death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:12.880 --> 01:07:17.760
But with Paul’s new consciousness, in&nbsp;
the book, he can perceive the forces of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:17.760 --> 01:07:25.680
the storm – the turbulence, the vortexes, all the&nbsp;
tiny gusts and fronts. He feels all these forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:25.680 --> 01:07:32.000
and sees the right path to fly to safety.
In the movie, Paul has a vision of this Fremen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:32.000 --> 01:07:39.520
man Jamis. Jamis says to understand a process,&nbsp;
you must flow with it. This is basically Dune’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:39.520 --> 01:07:45.520
philosophy of how to think – the world is&nbsp;
complicated, everything’s interconnected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:45.520 --> 01:07:52.400
ever changing. So you can’t just focus on one&nbsp;
perspective or one set of data. You can’t rely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:52.400 --> 01:07:59.360
on one narrow plan like Kynes did. Your thinking&nbsp;
needs to continually evolve with the changing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:59.360 --> 01:08:07.200
flow of reality. And that’s how Paul and Jessica&nbsp;
survive the storm, by seeing and moving with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:07.200 --> 01:08:13.760
flow. Just as Jessica and Paul master their&nbsp;
fear by allowing it to flow through them.&nbsp;

01:08:13.760 --> 01:08:18.720
In the movie, Paul hears the voices of&nbsp;
Bene Gesserits telling him to “let go”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:24.960
and so he lets go of the thopter controls,&nbsp;
and loses consciousness and somehow survives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:24.960 --> 01:08:30.880
it’s like an act of faith in the movie.&nbsp;
But the Dune book is not about faith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:30.880 --> 01:08:36.560
it’s not about losing consciousness. It’s&nbsp;
about awareness and choice, and so that’s how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:36.560 --> 01:08:42.560
Paul and Jessica fly to safety in the book.
The Harkonnens think Paul and Jessica died&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:42.560 --> 01:08:48.720
in the storm, and that their victory is finally&nbsp;
complete. So the Baron puts Rabban back in charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.720 --> 01:08:54.720
of Arrakis, and he tells him to “squeeze” – to&nbsp;
exploit and oppress the people of Arrakis to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:54.720 --> 01:09:01.600
as much money as possible, to make back the money&nbsp;
they spent on their attack. The “Beast” Rabban is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:01.600 --> 01:09:08.000
happy to be a brutal dictator again. In the book&nbsp;
he enthusiastically suggests exterminating every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:08.000 --> 01:09:14.240
person on Arrakis, and asks which weapons he’s&nbsp;
allowed to use on the population. The Baron just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:14.240 --> 01:09:20.240
orders Rabban to kill all the Fremen. In the book,&nbsp;
it’s the Sardaukar who do that. But in the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:20.240 --> 01:09:26.080
Rabban says it might not be easy to kill the&nbsp;
Fremen. In the book, we see the Fremen kick the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:26.080 --> 01:09:31.520
Sardaukar’s ass a couple times, and characters&nbsp;
are shocked by how deadly the Fremen are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:31.520 --> 01:09:36.960
But the Baron doesn’t listen, and arrogantly&nbsp;
dismisses the Fremen as an irrelevant rabble.&nbsp;

01:09:37.600 --> 01:09:43.520
Rabban is afraid of the Baron – the book says&nbsp;
the Baron usually won’t kill family members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:43.520 --> 01:09:48.160
but he does inflict “painful”&nbsp;
punishments. So Rabban obeys.&nbsp;

01:09:48.720 --> 01:09:52.160
In the book, there’s a lot more&nbsp;
going on in the Harkonnen plotline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:52.160 --> 01:09:56.400
with other important characters, which&nbsp;
we should see in the next Dune movie.&nbsp;

01:09:56.960 --> 01:10:02.640
Paul and Jessica travel the desert. Living&nbsp;
off their own water in stillsuits, they begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:02.640 --> 01:10:08.480
a true Arrakeen existence. The rhythm of&nbsp;
the environment shapes their movements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:08.480 --> 01:10:15.120
changes when they sleep, changes how they think.&nbsp;
They get a feel for the textures of the desert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:15.120 --> 01:10:22.400
all the different kinds of sand – pea sand,&nbsp;
drum sand, flour and grit and dust and powder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:22.400 --> 01:10:28.800
The desert looks like an “ocean”, “full of&nbsp;
moon-silvered waves”. Author Frank Herbert got the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:28.800 --> 01:10:34.880
idea for Dune when he was researching sand dunes&nbsp;
in Oregon. He learned that dunes move and flow&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:34.880 --> 01:10:41.360
like water, just slower. That began his research&nbsp;
into environments, and history and religions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:41.360 --> 01:10:46.480
linguistics that led to Dune.
Paul starts to like Arrakis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:46.480 --> 01:10:54.240
But Jessica just misses Caladan, and misses Leto.
In the book, Jessica gets buried under a sand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:54.240 --> 01:11:01.040
slide. So she uses her Bene Gesserit training to&nbsp;
hold her body in a coma so she doesn’t suffocate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:01.040 --> 01:11:07.520
while Paul uses his powerful mind to figure out&nbsp;
where she’s buried, and saves her. He does a cute&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:07.520 --> 01:11:14.000
chemistry experiment, mixing battery acid with&nbsp;
spice to make a foam to hold back the sand. It’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:14.000 --> 01:11:20.320
fun little science-fiction problem-solving scene,&nbsp;
using Paul’s mind and simple tools to survive.&nbsp;

01:11:20.960 --> 01:11:26.320
To find the Fremen, Paul and Jessica have&nbsp;
to cross an expanse of open sand. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:26.320 --> 01:11:34.000
is super dangerous because of the deadly worms.&nbsp;
Sandworms swim these sands like sharks in the sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:34.000 --> 01:11:40.240
But Paul and Jessica face this danger just like&nbsp;
the Old Duke faced the bull. Author Frank Herbert&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:40.240 --> 01:11:46.960
said sandworms are like the bull in the arena, or&nbsp;
the archetypal dragon in the cave, guarding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:46.960 --> 01:11:54.240
treasure of spice – the worm is the monster that&nbsp;
the hero must face. To avoid attracting a worm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:54.240 --> 01:12:00.800
they have to “walk without rhythm” – a sandwalk&nbsp;
that imitates the natural sounds of the desert.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:00.800 --> 01:12:06.480
In the book, they also set a thumper to distract&nbsp;
the worms. And it turns out it’s very difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:06.480 --> 01:12:13.360
to walk without rhythm while you can hear the&nbsp;
rhythm of the thumper. It’s yet another test of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:13.360 --> 01:12:21.200
control over the body and mind. Paul accidentally&nbsp;
steps on drum sand, which makes louds vibrations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:21.200 --> 01:12:27.040
and it summons a worm, so they’ve gotta leg&nbsp;
it to the safety of rock. Paul and Jessica are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:27.040 --> 01:12:34.080
shocked by the size of the worm. In the book,&nbsp;
its mouth is eighty metres or 260 feet across,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:34.080 --> 01:12:40.240
with crystal teeth glimmering the moonlight, and&nbsp;
breath that smells like cinnamon from the spice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:40.800 --> 01:12:46.080
Paul wonders about the mystery of exactly&nbsp;
how spice and sandworms are connected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:46.080 --> 01:12:51.920
The book mentions that sandworms’ bodies create&nbsp;
oxygen which is how the air is breathable on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:51.920 --> 01:12:57.360
Arrakis without many plants making oxygen&nbsp;
like on Earth. But they survive the worm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:57.360 --> 01:13:03.920
and then they face Stilgar and his Fremen. And&nbsp;
we start to learn more about Fremen culture.&nbsp;

01:13:03.920 --> 01:13:10.320
In the book, Stilgar asks if Paul and Jessica are&nbsp;
“jinn”, which is an Arabic word for supernatural&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:10.320 --> 01:13:17.520
spirits. Stilgar mentions “Istislah”, an Islamic&nbsp;
term relating to the common good. Jessica thinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:17.520 --> 01:13:23.920
the Fremen speak “the language of Ilm and Fiqh”&nbsp;
– which are Arabic words for knowledge and law.&nbsp;

01:13:24.720 --> 01:13:30.800
Cause the Fremen and their language are loosely&nbsp;
inspired by real Arabic and Islamic cultures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:30.800 --> 01:13:36.240
Author Frank Herbert was particularly influenced&nbsp;
by a book called The Sabres of Paradise about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.240 --> 01:13:42.800
Muslims in the Caucasus – it describes a religious&nbsp;
warrior people who speak a “Chakobsa” language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:42.800 --> 01:13:50.160
and fight against imperial “Padishah” invaders,&nbsp;
who live in a “Sietch” or “tabr”. There are entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:50.160 --> 01:13:56.240
lines in Dune that are taken from this book.&nbsp;
Frank was also inspired by Lawrence of Arabia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:56.240 --> 01:14:01.040
a Western man who joined with Bedouin&nbsp;
desert tribes in their fight against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:01.040 --> 01:14:07.760
Ottoman Empire. Stilgar says the Fremen are “the&nbsp;
Ichwan Bedwine”, which sounds like “Bedouin”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:08.320 --> 01:14:13.200
Frank was also inspired by the people of the&nbsp;
Kalahari desert, and their careful use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:13.200 --> 01:14:17.280
water. As well as indigenous American groups. So&nbsp;
many different real cultures inspired the Fremen.&nbsp;

01:14:17.920 --> 01:14:22.720
In-universe, the Dune book says that&nbsp;
Fremen are descended from Sunni Muslims in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:22.720 --> 01:14:29.200
Egypt – “Misr” is Arabic for Egypt, and&nbsp;
“Nilotic al-Ourouba”, suggests the Nile and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:29.200 --> 01:14:35.840
Arabia. Over thousands of years, this peoples’&nbsp;
religion changed into the Zensunni tradition – a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:35.840 --> 01:14:42.400
combination of Zen Buddhism with Sunni Islam.&nbsp;
The Zensunni Wanderers suffered millennia of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:42.400 --> 01:14:48.880
persecution and displacement, including nine&nbsp;
generations of slavery on Selusa Secundus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:48.880 --> 01:14:54.800
Eventually they settled on Arrakis, and developed&nbsp;
the unique Fremen culture, adapted to life in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:54.800 --> 01:15:01.200
desert, and hardened by their suffering.
Survival on Arrakis can require brutal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:01.200 --> 01:15:08.080
decisions for the good of the group – so Stilgar’s&nbsp;
Fremen threaten to kill Jessica to take her water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:08.080 --> 01:15:14.480
So Jessica uses her Bene Gesserit fighting skills&nbsp;
to kick Stilgar’s arse. And Paul beats up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:14.480 --> 01:15:21.440
Fremen man Jamis. So Jessica proves that she has&nbsp;
useful skills, and Stilgar agrees to keep her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:21.440 --> 01:15:28.240
In the book, he has some fun dialogue calling one&nbsp;
of his men a “wormfaced, crawling, sand-brained&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:28.240 --> 01:15:35.600
piece of lizard turd!”. Stilgar is charismatic,&nbsp;
and a powerful leader, dedicated to protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:35.600 --> 01:15:43.040
his tribe. He’s also very intelligent, and willing&nbsp;
to adapt to change. He asks Jessica to teach his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:43.040 --> 01:15:48.640
Fremen how to fight in her “weirding way”. And&nbsp;
the Fremen will teach Jessica and Paul to survive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:48.640 --> 01:15:55.680
in the desert. It will be “a good exchange of&nbsp;
teachings” – Dune values knowledge and learning.&nbsp;

01:15:56.480 --> 01:16:02.400
In the book, Jessica does more of her Missionaria&nbsp;
Protectiva manipulations, trying to convince the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:02.400 --> 01:16:08.960
Fremen that she and Paul fit the Lisan al-Gaib&nbsp;
prophecy. Jessica brings up Sietch Tabr, Stilgar’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:08.960 --> 01:16:14.800
home, as though she has some mystical knowledge of&nbsp;
the place – but really she just read the name on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:14.800 --> 01:16:22.800
map once – “If only he knew the tricks we use!”,&nbsp;
she thinks. Jessica feels a “cynical bitterness”&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:22.800 --> 01:16:29.920
at her manipulation. But she’s determined to use&nbsp;
the Fremen as an army to help Paul retake Arrakis.&nbsp;

01:16:30.720 --> 01:16:37.120
Paul meets Chani, and he’s shocked to recognise&nbsp;
her from his visions. In the books she has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:37.120 --> 01:16:44.240
an “elfin face”, a laughing voice, the grace&nbsp;
of a gazelle, and she has “red hair”. Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:44.240 --> 01:16:49.680
feels embarrassed that she caught him with a&nbsp;
gun, and he immediately has a crush on her.&nbsp;

01:16:50.400 --> 01:16:57.600
Jamis is angry that Paul beat him up. So Jamis&nbsp;
challenges Paul, invoking the amtal rule.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:57.600 --> 01:17:03.920
Amtal means to test something until it breaks,&nbsp;
to learn someone’s true nature by pushing them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:03.920 --> 01:17:10.800
to their limits, like the gom jabbar – Jamis&nbsp;
will fight Paul to the death. In the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:10.800 --> 01:17:17.040
Jamis says this will test whether Paul and&nbsp;
Jessica are the Lisan al-Gaib. He argues that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:17.040 --> 01:17:23.280
Jessica might be making “a false way among us”,&nbsp;
trying to deceive the Fremen to fit the prophecy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:23.280 --> 01:17:28.960
which.. she is. In the book, Jessica uses&nbsp;
the Voice on Jamis, and threatens him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:28.960 --> 01:17:36.240
but the Fremen realise she’s manipulating him and&nbsp;
order her to be silent. So Paul faces Jamis alone.&nbsp;

01:17:37.440 --> 01:17:44.160
Paul has seen possible futures from this&nbsp;
moment, and in most of them, Paul dies. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:44.160 --> 01:17:50.320
is at the centre of a nexus in time, a storm&nbsp;
of possibilities, where the slightest mistake&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:50.320 --> 01:17:56.400
could change the future dramatically. There are&nbsp;
too many branching possibilities for Paul to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:56.400 --> 01:18:03.600
a clear path to victory. The only way to survive&nbsp;
this is with his skills, his mind, and his knife.&nbsp;

01:18:04.320 --> 01:18:08.160
In the book, Chani gives Paul&nbsp;
advice on how to beat Jamis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:08.160 --> 01:18:14.320
warning him about Jamis’ fighting style. But in&nbsp;
the movie, Chani says she doesn’t believe in Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:14.320 --> 01:18:19.920
and that he’s gonna die. Which is a fun&nbsp;
change – making Chani more sceptical of Paul.&nbsp;

01:18:20.560 --> 01:18:25.840
In the movie, Chani gives Paul a crysknife&nbsp;
– and this is the same knife that Paul kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:25.840 --> 01:18:33.280
seeing in visions. Paul saw Chani stabbing him&nbsp;
with this knife. Will this vision come true?&nbsp;

01:18:34.240 --> 01:18:41.440
Paul salutes Jamis in the same way that Duncan&nbsp;
saluted earlier – an echo of his fallen friend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:41.440 --> 01:18:44.320
And they prepare to fight. In the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:44.320 --> 01:18:49.840
Paul and Jamis fight wearing just loincloths.&nbsp;
But in the movie they wear their stillsuits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:49.840 --> 01:18:53.840
so we don’t get to see Timothee&nbsp;
Chalamet in just his “fighting trunks”.&nbsp;

01:18:54.400 --> 01:18:59.600
In the movie, Paul hears the voices of&nbsp;
Bene Gesserit telling him not to be afraid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:59.600 --> 01:19:06.480
not to resist, to fulfil his destiny as the&nbsp;
Kwisatz Haderach. But in the book, Paul doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:06.480 --> 01:19:14.160
rely on destiny – he focusses on his training. On&nbsp;
the advice of Jessica, Duncan and Gurney. On his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:14.160 --> 01:19:21.280
Bene Gesserit mastery of his body and mind. Paul&nbsp;
is “a fighting machine” trained from infancy by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:21.280 --> 01:19:26.800
some of the best warriors in the universe.
But in the book, Paul’s training gives him&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:26.800 --> 01:19:32.880
a disadvantage. Cause Paul is trained for&nbsp;
shield-fighting. He learned to attack slowly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:32.880 --> 01:19:39.440
so his blade can penetrate shields. But Jamis&nbsp;
has no shield. The Fremen don’t use shields,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:39.440 --> 01:19:46.160
because shields attract sandworms. So Paul&nbsp;
keeps attacking Jamis too slowly. While Jamis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:46.160 --> 01:19:51.120
who isn’t trained with shields, attacks&nbsp;
faster than Paul is used to. So Paul has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:51.120 --> 01:19:57.040
to change his fighting style. Sometimes to&nbsp;
survive, you have to unlearn old assumptions.&nbsp;

01:19:58.080 --> 01:20:05.040
So Paul kills Jamis, winning the respect of&nbsp;
the Fremen. Jessica is relieved that her son&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:05.040 --> 01:20:10.960
survived. But in the book, she does something&nbsp;
interesting. Jessica immediately comes up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:10.960 --> 01:20:18.880
calls Paul a killer, her voice harsh and full&nbsp;
of scorn. She deliberately makes Paul feel bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:18.880 --> 01:20:26.160
about killing Jamis. Because Paul gets a lot&nbsp;
of attention and validation for this victory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:26.160 --> 01:20:33.120
including from the girl of his dreams, Chani.&nbsp;
Jessica doesn’t want Paul to learn to enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:33.120 --> 01:20:39.040
killing. So she applies some emotional&nbsp;
conditioning to keep Paul’s morality in line.&nbsp;

01:20:40.080 --> 01:20:46.000
In the movie, there’s a sadness to Jamis’&nbsp;
death. Cause earlier, Paul had visions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:46.000 --> 01:20:52.240
Jamis being a “friend”, someone who taught Paul&nbsp;
about the desert. But because of Paul’s choices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:52.240 --> 01:20:57.920
and chance – that vision didn’t come true, and&nbsp;
Paul had to kill the man who could have been his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:57.920 --> 01:21:04.400
friend. So Paul’s visions aren’t guaranteed, just&nbsp;
possibilities. They’re like predictions, plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:05.280 --> 01:21:11.600
hopes and fears in the real world – they can&nbsp;
guide us to achieve our goals, but they can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:12.800 --> 01:21:19.520
limit our thinking. Dune explores the power&nbsp;
and the danger of predictions and dreams.&nbsp;

01:21:20.320 --> 01:21:26.560
In the book, Paul cries for Jamis, and the&nbsp;
Fremen see crying as a profound sacred act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:26.560 --> 01:21:32.880
because of how precious water is in the desert.&nbsp;
Paul says that Jamis was his friend, that Jamis&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:32.880 --> 01:21:39.760
taught him that killing has a price. So maybe,&nbsp;
in a way, Paul’s vision of Jamis as a friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:39.760 --> 01:21:46.080
who taught him, did come true after all.
The Fremen wrap up Jamis’ body and take it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:46.080 --> 01:21:50.720
with them. Because when a Fremen dies,&nbsp;
the water is drained from their body&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:50.720 --> 01:21:56.800
in a “deathstill” and reclaimed for the Fremen&nbsp;
to use. “The flesh belongs to the person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:56.800 --> 01:22:02.320
but his water belongs to the tribe”.
In the movie, Paul gives a confident&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:02.320 --> 01:22:07.962
heroic speech. He says he sees his future,&nbsp;
and he chooses to be with the Fremen. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:07.962 --> 01:22:11.840
an uplifting and optimistic end to the movie. But&nbsp;
at this point in the book, the vibe is darker and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:11.840 --> 01:22:18.400
uncertain. Paul and Jessica are unsure about the&nbsp;
Fremen, but feel forced to use them to survive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:18.400 --> 01:22:25.360
Paul doesn’t see his future clearly – he feels&nbsp;
he is ‘plunged into the abyss’. He’s afraid of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:25.360 --> 01:22:32.320
his visions of the bloody Jihad, and desperately&nbsp;
hopes to prevent it. Paul’s destiny is dangerous.&nbsp;

01:22:33.040 --> 01:22:39.120
We see a Fremen riding a sandworm to travel –&nbsp;
there’s much more to learn about the power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:39.120 --> 01:22:45.440
the Fremen and their desert. Chani says “This&nbsp;
is only the beginning”, and it really is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:46.000 --> 01:22:51.840
This movie only covers the first half&nbsp;
of the first book in the Dune trilogy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:51.840 --> 01:22:57.600
Which itself is just the beginning of a larger&nbsp;
series. There is so much more to this story.&nbsp;

01:22:58.320 --> 01:23:04.720
Frank Herbert said that the first Dune book has a&nbsp;
“coital” rhythm. Meaning the pacing of the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:04.720 --> 01:23:10.960
is like sex – it starts slow, and speeds&nbsp;
up, then suddenly climactically ends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:11.520 --> 01:23:17.920
So by ending this movie halfway through the&nbsp;
first book, Denis Villeneuve stops mid-coitus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:17.920 --> 01:23:22.400
leaving us with waiting, without a&nbsp;
climax, until Dune Part Two comes out.&nbsp;

01:23:23.040 --> 01:23:29.440
This movie is a mostly faithful adaptation of&nbsp;
the first half of the first book. It introduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:29.440 --> 01:23:36.080
the worlds and politics of the Imperium, the&nbsp;
mysterious Spacing Guild, the scheming Bene&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:36.080 --> 01:23:42.240
Gesserit. The evil Harkonnens defeat the Atreides,&nbsp;
Paul and Jessica escape to the Fremen, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:42.240 --> 01:23:49.920
start to learn the ways of the desert. We see&nbsp;
Paul’s complicated destiny and ambiguous visions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:49.920 --> 01:23:56.800
and hints of the dangers of the messiah. The&nbsp;
movie introduces ideas of the environment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:56.800 --> 01:24:03.520
religion and political exploitation and the&nbsp;
power of the mind. But the movie leaves out some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:03.520 --> 01:24:10.240
important scenes like the dinner party, and the&nbsp;
traitor subplot. It doesn’t capture the tragedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:10.240 --> 01:24:18.240
of Doctor Yueh, the meaning of Kynes’ death, or&nbsp;
the darker manipulative side of Leto and Jessica.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:18.240 --> 01:24:23.840
It removes some of the Arabic references from&nbsp;
the Fremen, and it skips plotlines with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:23.840 --> 01:24:29.040
Harkonnens and with Thufir. Hopefully&nbsp;
we’ll get this in the next Dune movie.&nbsp;

01:24:29.040 --> 01:24:34.640
But to understand the meaning of Paul’s story,&nbsp;
and the message of Dune, we’ve got to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:34.640 --> 01:24:41.280
about the other books. So we’ve got a new video&nbsp;
called the Philosophy of Dune, coming out soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:41.280 --> 01:24:47.760
We’ve also got a Podcast episode out now talking&nbsp;
about Dune with Quinn, from Quinn’s Ideas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:47.760 --> 01:24:54.800
We’re also doing a Q&amp;A livestream about Dune on&nbsp;
this channel in 24 hours. And for some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:54.800 --> 01:25:00.880
professional shitposter Alt Schwift X has&nbsp;
released a rap about Dune. So check out all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:00.880 --> 01:25:07.520
that Dune content linked below. We will make more&nbsp;
